<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William III of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1650">1650</a>–<a href="/years/1702">1702</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as William III of Orange, William of Orange · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William III, Prince of Orange, became stadtholder of Holland in 1672, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, and led the resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>. In 1688 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and in 1689 he and his wife Mary II were proclaimed joint monarchs of England, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and Ireland. He led the coalition against France in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a>, retaking Namur in 1695, and ruled until his death in 1702.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-england.jpg" alt="William III of England" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Godfrey Kneller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_William_III_of_England,_(1650-1702).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William was born on 4 November 1650 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, the only child of the stadtholder William II, Prince of Orange, who died before his birth, and Mary, Princess Royal, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england">Charles I of England</a>. His mother took little personal interest in him, and from April 1656 the Calvinist preacher Cornelis Trigland gave him daily instruction in the Reformed religion. After his father's death most provinces left the office of stadtholder vacant.</p><p>In 1672, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rampjaar">Rampjaar</a> or disaster year, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and its allies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Münster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, and the French army overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelderland">Gelderland</a> and Utrecht. William withdrew into Holland, where the dikes were flooded, and the people turned against de Witt and his allies. William became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s chief opponent and reorganised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> army. In 1677 he married his first cousin Mary, elder daughter of the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-maine">York</a>, later James II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>James's reign was unpopular with British Protestants. In June 1688 the birth of a son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-francis-edward-stuart">James Francis Edward Stuart</a>, displaced Mary from first place in the succession. William, invited by leading English Protestants, landed at Brixham in November 1688 with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> force, and James was deposed. A Convention Parliament met on 22 January 1689. William insisted on reigning as king in his own right, and he and Mary were offered the crown jointly. Parliament passed the Toleration Act 1689 and, in December 1689, the Bill of Rights, which limited the royal prerogative. William disliked such limits but agreed to abide by the statute.</p><p>A minority, the Jacobites, refused to recognise William, and over 400 clergy, the nonjurors, would not take oaths of allegiance. He spent each campaigning season abroad in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, while Mary governed. She died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> on 28 December 1694. The Treaty of Rijswijk of 20 September 1697 ended the war, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> recognised William as king. After the death in 1700 of Anne's last surviving child, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement 1701</a> named Sophia, Electress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, and her Protestant heirs as heirs to the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>William died at Kensington Palace on 8 March 1702, aged 51, of pneumonia after a fall from his horse broke his collarbone. Some Jacobites toasted the mole whose burrow had tripped the horse as the little gentleman in the black velvet waistcoat. He was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> beside Mary, and Anne succeeded him. Contemporaries, even his enemies, praised him as a field commander. In Ulster he is called King Billy, and his victory at the Boyne in 1690 is commemorated each year in the Twelfth of July parades of the Orange Order.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William III of England” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>William III</b> it then led coalitions against Louis XIV in the Nine Years’ War and the War of the Spanish Succession, wars whose cost left Holland heavily indebted.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Williamite War of 1689–1691 the Protestant city of Derry withstood a siege of 105 days, <b>William III</b> defeated James II at the Battle of the Boyne in 1690, and the Battle of Aughrim in 1691 preceded the Treaty of Limerick.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His son, Elector Frederick III, wanted a royal crown to match the elevation of the Elector of Saxony to the Polish throne and of <b>William of Orange</b> to the English one.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>William III of England</b>, also prince of Orange, died in 1702, Lingen passed to his Prussian heir Frederick I and became part of the Kingdom of Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-lingen">County of Lingen</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis XIV took the citadel in 1692 and <b>William III</b> retook it in 1695.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-namur">County of Namur</a></li><li><blockquote><b>William III</b>, wary of offending Spain, forbade English colonies to supply them.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-caledonia-darien">New Caledonia (Darien)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binnenhof">Binnenhof</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.08,4.31&amp;year=1683" aria-label="Binnenhof on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4647 364 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William III of England"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5097 634h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5097 634h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.08,4.31&amp;year=1683">What was here in 1683? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
